As I understand it, they photobleach with cleared samples (RI matched in BABB), then rehydrate, immunostain, and clear again for imaging. I guess there's no theoretical reason why the photobleaching shouldn't work in aqueous solution. Not sure how the SHIELD fixation and H2O2 would interact though. 

(I did something similar a while ago with a much simpler device, just wrapped a string of UV LEDs around a beaker and placed 5ml eppies with cleared mouse brains in DBE in there for 24h. Worked well enough to reduce background autofluo, but I guess there's a lot of room for improvement). 

@SKD, yes that would make sense! 0.1% H2O2 would also mean roughly 0.2% water (if stock is 30% H2O2 in water, it's roughly a 1:2 mix of H2O2/water) in the solution, not sure how much of an issue that would pose with DBE or ECi. Should be easy enough to test, I figure :)

