Hi Helena and Rob, a bit late, also here my comments; @RL: excellent overview of commercial L-sheet options! On our side (The Crick) we have here the Miltenyi-Lavision UMII and we have intensively tested (1 month ago) the Blaze Miltenyi; I could speak for hours regarding both…
Briefly, Lavision UMII: easy and very flexible (large chamber, wide zoom range), but a bit tricky for alignment and high res imaging;
 Blaze: is an improvement (except for the crazy large cuvette) and can be used for multisamples and bigger samples (lenses with higher WD); some improvements in the image quality (flat field of lenses and organization of 3 light-sheet foci;);
 in general, they both still suffer from not very high imaging speed and issues in aligning 6 L-sheets on the same plane; if you need more info, I`ll be happy to set a Zoom meeting (maybe also open to other people?)  and if you want to test the Lavision UMII, you are welcome here in London; or if you have more specific questions, please let me know; hope it helps!
 @ Rob: good to know you have the 3i; we are interested in it as well I`ll be happy to hear your feedback

Hi Sylvain,
I did some imaging of beads with our Light-sheets (MuVi Luxendo and Lavision UMII)and to be honest, I don`t see your PSFs so bad; looking at the PSF from some confocal, I remember seeing patterns similar to what you show here;  I suppose that a critical info is where was the beam waist (if Gaussian..) and the center of the det lens in relation to the beads; I generally see many aberrations when imaging on the outer part of detection lens (as expected) or with not well aligned sheets; I can send you some snapshots of beads image in ECi where PSF quality is very different in the FOV;
One test would be to see the distortion is coming from the detection or the illumination; maybe trying different sheet alignments, detection lens, ROI in the FOV? Hope it help and just let me know if you need more info; cheers, Alessandro

