@KB Yes, for us it seems to work just fine for mouse brains - I assume it's similar to the recent white-and-clear protocol where they found that blocking catalases yields better H2O2 bleaching efficicency - maybe catalase function is already reduced in 100% MeOH, and that's why it works better with what is arguably less H2O2 than in the iDISCO protocol.
@LH - good to know, I naïvely assumed this was a mouse brain. Two random ideas come to mind:
1.  If I read correctly, mpeg1 is an immune cell marker - I assume you checked that this isn't actual on-target staining, that the zebrafish from your new lab are somehow immune-stressed and there are more immune cells at the outside of the brain? You should see this just from the eGFP fluorescence in unbleached samples, I assume. 
2. (Or that you got a bad batch of nanobodies, maybe in your old lab they were diluted more upon storage and now you have the correct, but higher concentration)? This should be verifiable by trying a different batch of nanobodies.
